IMPORT prelude

-- Oaths, Affidavits and Statutory Declarations Act 2005 (WA) -- who may witness my WA statutory declaration or my
-- affidavit, how must it be made and witnessed, what does the Act say about doing it remotely, and what happens if what
-- I declare is false?
--
-- SEAT: the declarant (the maker of a statutory declaration or an affidavit), and the lawyer asked to act as authorised
-- witness. Every decision is phrased as one of them asks it, from facts they could state. Where the Act speaks to someone
-- else (the court that receives the document, s 14 and s 15(3); the chief judicial officer, s 10), the encoding says what
-- it means for the declarant or the witness.
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 5 (affirming instead of swearing), s 7(3) (oaths and affirmations by audio or video link),
-- s 8 ("experienced legal practitioner"), s 9(6)-(8) (who is an authorised witness for an affidavit in and outside WA,
-- the s 9(7) exclusion) and s 9(3)(c) (the presence requirement, for the remote question only), s 10(5) (court authorised witnesses), s 11 (anyone may make a statutory
-- declaration), s 12 (statutory declarations: Schedule 1 form, how made, the witness's duties, who is an authorised
-- witness in WA, elsewhere in Australia and abroad), s 13 (blind or illiterate makers), s 14 (makers not conversant with
-- English), s 15 (rubber stamps), s 16 (non-compliance with form or procedure), s 17 (pretending to be an authorised
-- witness), Schedule 1 (the form), Schedule 2 (authorised witnesses for statutory declarations, as data).
-- BROUGHT IN: the Criminal Code (WA) s 169 (false statement on oath; false statement in a statutory declaration), because
-- this Act creates no false-declaration offence of its own and Schedule 1 makes the declarant recite one. The
-- Interpretation Act 1984 (WA) s 5 meanings of "lawyer" and "legal practitioner", which point to the Legal Profession
-- Uniform Law (WA) s 6(1).
--
-- Edition: the WA compilation including amendments to the Legal Profession Uniform Law Application Act 2022 (No. 9 of 2022)
-- s 424, in force 1 July 2022, with the Evidence Act 2025 s 492(2) shown as uncommenced, as deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_48924.txt (retrieved 2026-09-07). Criminal Code s 169 as deposited at
-- ../../../criminal-code-act-compilation-act-1913/registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_49336.txt (retrieved 2026-09-07).
-- Interpretation Act 1984 s 5 as deposited at ../../../interpretation-act-1984/registers/source-bundle/; Legal Profession
-- Uniform Law (WA) s 6(1) as deposited at ../../../legal-profession-uniform-law-wa/registers/source-bundle/.
-- No regulations under s 18 are deposited: whether an official is a "prescribed consular official" under s 3(c) is an
-- INPUT. The Statutory Declarations Regulations 2023 (Cth) are not deposited: whether a person is one before whom a
-- Commonwealth statutory declaration may be made (s 12(6)(a)(ii), (b)(ii)) is an INPUT.
--
-- NOT ENCODED: the forms of oath (s 4) beyond s 5; who administers oaths in court (s 6(1)) and the s 7(1)-(2) ritual;
-- the affidavit's own form, signing and witnessing limbs (s 9(1)-(5)) and s 9(9); the appointment and cessation
-- machinery of court authorised witnesses (s 10(2)-(4)); regulations (ss 12(7), 18);
-- commencement. The COVID-19 modification of s 9 (endnote 1M) is not in the deposited text; endnote 1M says it
-- "ceases on 31 December 2021 unless postponed". The Evidence Act 2025 amendment (uncommenced) is not read. The Criminal Code's general
-- excuses (Chapter V) and the choice between indictment and summary conviction are not encoded.
--
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Written with the writing-l4-rules skill from the individual's seat with the design defaults ruled on
-- 2026-10-18. No pipeline, no coverage table, no independent test pass, no human gate. NOT for public use as advice.

§ `Oaths, Affidavits and Statutory Declarations Act 2005`

§§ `5 -- may I affirm instead of swearing`

-- s 5(1): "If under this Act or any other law ... a person has to or may take an oath, the person is entitled to affirm
-- instead." s 5(3): "An affirmation made instead of an oath has the same force and effect as the oath."
@export May I affirm instead of swearing an oath, for example for my affidavit (s 5)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I affirm instead of swearing` IS TRUE

§§ `11 -- may I make a statutory declaration`

-- s 11(1): "A person may make a statutory declaration about any matter at any time, for any purpose and without the need
-- for any legal authority to do so other than this section."
@export May I make a WA statutory declaration about any matter, at any time, for any purpose (s 11)?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I make a statutory declaration about any matter` IS TRUE

§§ `Interpretation Act s 5 and s 8 -- lawyers and experienced legal practitioners`

-- Interpretation Act 1984 s 5: "lawyer means an Australian lawyer as defined in the Legal Profession Uniform Law (WA)
-- section 6(1)"; "legal practitioner means an Australian legal practitioner as defined in" the same section.
-- Uniform Law s 6(1): "Australian lawyer means a person admitted to the Australian legal profession in this jurisdiction
-- or any other jurisdiction"; "Australian legal practitioner means an Australian lawyer who holds a current Australian
-- practising certificate". Admission and the practising certificate are borrowed terms, asked as facts.
-- s 8: "experienced legal practitioner means a person who is, and has been for at least 2 years, a legal practitioner."
@export Am I a "lawyer" within item 20 of Schedule 2 (Interpretation Act s 5)?
GIVEN `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been admitted as a lawyer in any Australian jurisdiction
      `I hold a current Australian practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you hold a current practising certificate (not needed to be a lawyer)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I a lawyer` IF
    `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession`

@export Am I an "experienced legal practitioner" for affidavits (s 8)?
GIVEN `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have been admitted as a lawyer in any Australian jurisdiction
      `I hold a current Australian practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you hold a current practising certificate now
      `years I have been a legal practitioner` IS A NUMBER @desc For how many years, up to now, you have continuously been a lawyer holding a current practising certificate
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Am I an experienced legal practitioner` IF
        `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession`
    AND `I hold a current Australian practising certificate`
    AND `years I have been a legal practitioner` AT LEAST 2

§§ `Schedule 2 -- authorised witnesses for statutory declarations made in WA`

-- s 12(6)(a)(i): "any person described in the second column of Schedule 2". The table is held as data: one constructor
-- per item, named by its informal description (third column), with the item number. The formal description (second
-- column) is what counts; it is given in the @desc of the question below and in the comments here. Where an item carries
-- its own condition, a constructor for the person who falls short of it is added so that the condition is visible.
DECLARE `Schedule 2 description` IS ONE OF
    `Academic (postsecondary institution) (item 1)`  -- academic staff of Curtin, ECU, Murdoch, Notre Dame, UWA or a VET Act institution
    `Accountant (item 2)`                             -- a member of ATMA, CPA Australia, ICAA, IPA or NTAA
    `Architect (item 3)`
    `Australian Consular Officer (item 4)`
    `Australian Diplomatic Officer (item 5)`
    `Bailiff (item 6)`
    `Bank manager (item 7)`                           -- in charge of the head office or a branch of an ADI
    `Chartered secretary, governance adviser or risk manager (item 8)`
    `Chemist (item 9)`
    `Chiropractor (item 10)`
    `Company auditor or liquidator (item 11)`
    `Court officer (item 12)`                         -- judge, master, magistrate, registrar, clerk or CEO of a State or Commonwealth court
    `Defence force officer (item 13)`                 -- an officer, a warrant officer, or an NCO with 5 or more years' continuous service
    `Dentist (item 14)`
    `Doctor (item 15)`
    `Electorate officer of a member of State Parliament (item 15A)`
    `Engineer (item 16)`                              -- a member of the Institution of Engineers, Australia, "other than at the grade of student"
    `Industrial organisation secretary (item 17)`
    `Insurance broker (item 18)`
    `Justice of the Peace (item 19)`
    `Landgate officer (item 19A)`
    `Lawyer (item 20)`
    `Local government CEO or deputy CEO (item 21)`
    `Local government councillor (item 22)`
    `Loss adjuster (item 23)`
    `Marriage celebrant (item 24)`
    `Member of Parliament (item 25)`
    `Midwife (item 25A)`
    `Minister of religion (item 26)`
    `Nurse (item 27)`
    `Optometrist (item 28)`
    `Paramedic (item 28A)`
    `Patent attorney (item 29)`
    `Physiotherapist (item 30)`
    `Podiatrist (item 31)`
    `Police officer (item 32)`
    `Post office manager (item 33)`
    `Psychologist (item 34)`
    `Public notary (item 35)`
    `Public servant (Commonwealth) (item 36)`
    `Public servant (State) (item 37)`
    `Real estate agent (item 38)`
    `Settlement agent (item 39)`
    `Sheriff or deputy sheriff (item 40)`
    `Surveyor (item 41)`
    `Registered teacher (item 42)`
    `Tribunal officer (item 43)`
    `Veterinarian (item 44)`
    `a non-commissioned officer with fewer than 5 years' continuous service (falls short of item 13)`
    `a student-grade member of the Institution of Engineers (falls short of item 16)`
    `none of the Schedule 2 descriptions`

GIVEN `d` IS A `Schedule 2 description`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `is a Schedule 2 witness` IF
    CONSIDER `d`
    WHEN `a non-commissioned officer with fewer than 5 years' continuous service (falls short of item 13)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `a student-grade member of the Institution of Engineers (falls short of item 16)` THEN FALSE
    WHEN `none of the Schedule 2 descriptions` THEN FALSE
    OTHERWISE TRUE

§§ `12(6) -- who may witness my WA statutory declaration`

-- s 12(6): "An authorised witness for a statutory declaration is -- (a) for a statutory declaration that is made at a
-- place in Western Australia -- (i) any person described in the second column of Schedule 2; or (ii) any person before
-- whom, under the Statutory Declarations Act 1959 of the Commonwealth, a statutory declaration may be made; (b) for a
-- statutory declaration that is made at a place outside Western Australia but within Australia -- (i) any person who,
-- under the law of that place, has authority to take or receive a statutory, solemn or other declaration; or (ii) [the
-- Commonwealth Act limb]; (c) for a statutory declaration that is made at any other place -- (i) a prescribed consular
-- official who is performing official functions at that place; (ii) a person who is a justice or notary public under the
-- law of that place; (iii) a person who has authority under the law of that place to administer an oath to another
-- person or to take, receive or witness a statutory, solemn or other declaration."
-- s 3: "prescribed consular official" is an Australian Consular or Diplomatic Officer (Consular Fees Act 1955 (Cth)), a
-- British consul or vice consul, or an official the regulations prescribe. Asked as a fact.
DECLARE `Place` IS ONE OF
    `in Western Australia`
    `elsewhere in Australia`
    `outside Australia`

DECLARE `Statutory declaration witness` HAS
    `where the declaration is made` IS A `Place`
    `my Schedule 2 description` IS A `Schedule 2 description`
    @desc Which description in the second column of Schedule 2 fits you, if any
    `I am a Commonwealth statutory declaration witness` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether a Commonwealth statutory declaration may be made before you under the Statutory Declarations Act 1959 (its Regulations are not deposited)
    `I have authority under the law of that place to take declarations` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the law of the place where it is made gives you authority to take, receive or witness a statutory, solemn or other declaration (or, abroad, to administer an oath)
    `I am a prescribed consular official working there` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you are a prescribed consular official (s 3) performing official functions at that place
    `I am a justice or notary public under the law of that place` IS A BOOLEAN

DECLARE `My standing as a statutory declaration witness` IS ONE OF
    `authorised: a person described in Schedule 2 (s 12(6)(a)(i))`
    `authorised: a Commonwealth statutory declaration witness (s 12(6)(a)(ii) or (b)(ii))`
    `authorised: authority under the law of the Australian place where it is made (s 12(6)(b)(i))`
    `authorised: a prescribed consular official working there (s 12(6)(c)(i))`
    `authorised: a justice or notary public of that place (s 12(6)(c)(ii))`
    `authorised: authority under the law of that place outside Australia (s 12(6)(c)(iii))`
    `not an authorised witness for a declaration made at that place`

@export May I witness this WA statutory declaration, and on what footing (s 12(6), Schedule 2)?
GIVEN `me` IS A `Statutory declaration witness` @desc Where the declaration is made and who you are
GIVETH A `My standing as a statutory declaration witness`
DECIDE `May I witness this statutory declaration` IS
    CONSIDER `me`'s `where the declaration is made`
    WHEN `in Western Australia` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `is a Schedule 2 witness` (`me`'s `my Schedule 2 description`)
                THEN `authorised: a person described in Schedule 2 (s 12(6)(a)(i))`
            IF `me`'s `I am a Commonwealth statutory declaration witness`
                THEN `authorised: a Commonwealth statutory declaration witness (s 12(6)(a)(ii) or (b)(ii))`
            OTHERWISE `not an authorised witness for a declaration made at that place`
    WHEN `elsewhere in Australia` THEN
        BRANCH
            IF `me`'s `I have authority under the law of that place to take declarations`
                THEN `authorised: authority under the law of the Australian place where it is made (s 12(6)(b)(i))`
            IF `me`'s `I am a Commonwealth statutory declaration witness`
                THEN `authorised: a Commonwealth statutory declaration witness (s 12(6)(a)(ii) or (b)(ii))`
            OTHERWISE `not an authorised witness for a declaration made at that place`
    OTHERWISE
        BRANCH
            IF `me`'s `I am a prescribed consular official working there`
                THEN `authorised: a prescribed consular official working there (s 12(6)(c)(i))`
            IF `me`'s `I am a justice or notary public under the law of that place`
                THEN `authorised: a justice or notary public of that place (s 12(6)(c)(ii))`
            IF `me`'s `I have authority under the law of that place to take declarations`
                THEN `authorised: authority under the law of that place outside Australia (s 12(6)(c)(iii))`
            OTHERWISE `not an authorised witness for a declaration made at that place`

§§ `9(6)-(8), 10(5) -- who may witness my affidavit`

-- s 9(6): "An authorised witness for an affidavit that is made at a place within Western Australia is -- (a) a Justice of
-- the Peace; or (b) an experienced legal practitioner, unless excluded by subsection (7); or (c) a public notary within
-- the meaning of the Public Notaries Act 1979; or (d) any person referred to in section 6(2)." s 6(2): "Any registrar or
-- clerk of a court, and any mining registrar appointed under the Mining Act 1978".
-- s 9(7): "An experienced legal practitioner who has participated in any way in preparing an affidavit, or in the
-- proceedings in which an affidavit is intended to be used, is not an authorised witness for the affidavit."
-- s 9(8): outside WA -- "(a) a judge of a court of that place, or a magistrate or justice of the peace of or for that
-- place; or (b) a notary public; or (c) a prescribed consular official who is performing official functions at that
-- place; or (d) a person who has authority under the law of that place to administer an oath to another person."
-- s 10(5): "a court authorised witness is an authorised witness for any affidavit that is made for use in the court for
-- which the officer is appointed". s 9(9) (another written law naming someone else) is not encoded.
DECLARE `Affidavit witness` HAS
    `the affidavit is made in Western Australia` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a Justice of the Peace` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I hold a current Australian practising certificate` IS A BOOLEAN
    `years I have been a legal practitioner` IS A NUMBER
    `I took part in preparing the affidavit or in its proceedings` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you participated in any way in preparing the affidavit, or in the proceedings it is meant for
    `I am a public notary` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Within the meaning of the Public Notaries Act 1979
    `I am a court registrar or clerk, or a mining registrar` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I am a court authorised witness for the court it is for` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether you are appointed a court authorised witness (s 10) for the court in which the affidavit is to be used
    `I am a person s 9(8) names for that place` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Outside WA: a judge, magistrate or justice of the peace of that place, a notary public, a prescribed consular official working there, or a person with authority under that law to administer an oath

DECLARE `My standing as an affidavit witness` IS ONE OF
    `authorised: a Justice of the Peace (s 9(6)(a))`
    `authorised: a public notary (s 9(6)(c))`
    `authorised: a court registrar or clerk, or a mining registrar (s 9(6)(d), s 6(2))`
    `authorised: a court authorised witness, for use in that court (s 10(5))`
    `authorised: an experienced legal practitioner (s 9(6)(b))`
    `not authorised: an experienced legal practitioner who took part in preparing it or in its proceedings (s 9(7))`
    `not authorised: not yet a legal practitioner for 2 years (s 8)`
    `authorised: a person s 9(8) names for the place outside WA`
    `not an authorised witness for this affidavit`

@export May I witness this affidavit, and on what footing (ss 8, 9(6)-(8), 10(5))?
GIVEN `me` IS A `Affidavit witness` @desc Where the affidavit is made, who you are, and whether you were involved in it
GIVETH A `My standing as an affidavit witness`
DECIDE `May I witness this affidavit` IS
    IF (NOT `me`'s `the affidavit is made in Western Australia`)
    THEN IF `me`'s `I am a person s 9(8) names for that place`
         THEN `authorised: a person s 9(8) names for the place outside WA`
         ELSE `not an authorised witness for this affidavit`
    ELSE
        BRANCH
            IF `me`'s `I am a Justice of the Peace`
                THEN `authorised: a Justice of the Peace (s 9(6)(a))`
            IF `me`'s `I am a public notary`
                THEN `authorised: a public notary (s 9(6)(c))`
            IF `me`'s `I am a court registrar or clerk, or a mining registrar`
                THEN `authorised: a court registrar or clerk, or a mining registrar (s 9(6)(d), s 6(2))`
            IF `me`'s `I am a court authorised witness for the court it is for`
                THEN `authorised: a court authorised witness, for use in that court (s 10(5))`
            IF `experienced` `me` AND `me`'s `I took part in preparing the affidavit or in its proceedings`
                THEN `not authorised: an experienced legal practitioner who took part in preparing it or in its proceedings (s 9(7))`
            IF `experienced` `me`
                THEN `authorised: an experienced legal practitioner (s 9(6)(b))`
            IF `me`'s `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession` AND `me`'s `I hold a current Australian practising certificate`
                THEN `not authorised: not yet a legal practitioner for 2 years (s 8)`
            OTHERWISE `not an authorised witness for this affidavit`
    WHERE
        `experienced` `w` MEANS
            `Am I an experienced legal practitioner` (`w`'s `I have been admitted to the Australian legal profession`)
                (`w`'s `I hold a current Australian practising certificate`)
                (`w`'s `years I have been a legal practitioner`)

§§ `12(2)-(5), 16 -- is my statutory declaration validly made`

-- s 12(2): "The statutory declaration must be in the form in Schedule 1."
-- s 12(3): the maker must "(a) sign or personally mark the statutory declaration; and (b) sign or initial any alteration
-- ...; and (c) in the presence of an authorised witness declare orally -- (i) that he or she is the person named as the
-- maker ...; (ii) that the contents ... are true; (iii) that the signature or mark is his or hers; and (iv) if necessary,
-- that any attachment ... is the attachment referred to in it." s 12(4): (a) and (b) "need not be complied with in the
-- presence of an authorised witness."
-- s 12(5): "After the maker has complied with subsection (3)(c), the authorised witness must -- (a) sign or personally
-- mark the statutory declaration; and (b) sign or initial any alteration ... signed or initialled by the maker; and (c)
-- imprint or clearly write his or her name and qualification as an authorised witness."
-- s 16(1): validity "is not affected by the fact that the person ... does not use the exact words required as long as
-- the words actually used do not materially affect the substance of the exact words and are not likely to mislead."
-- s 16(2): validity "is not affected by the fact that the required procedure ... is not followed exactly as long as the
-- procedure actually followed substantially complies with the required procedure." Whether s 16 saves a departure is a
-- judgment for whoever relies on the declaration (in the end, a court); it is an INPUT, answered conditionally.
DECLARE `Statutory declaration` HAS
    `it is in the Schedule 1 form` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the witness is an authorised witness` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the witness is authorised under s 12(6) for the place where it is made
    `I signed or personally marked it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `I signed or initialled every alteration` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc TRUE also if there were no alterations
    `I declared the s 12(3)(c) matters orally in the witness's presence` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc That you are the maker, the contents are true, the signature is yours, and any attachment is the one referred to
    `the witness then signed or personally marked it` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the witness signed or initialled my alterations` IS A BOOLEAN
    `the witness wrote their name and qualification` IS A BOOLEAN
    `any departure is saved by s 16` IS A BOOLEAN
    @desc Whether the words used do not materially differ and are not likely to mislead, and the procedure followed substantially complies

DECLARE `How my statutory declaration stands` IS ONE OF
    `validly made under s 12`
    `valid despite a departure: s 16 saves it`
    `not validly made: not in the Schedule 1 form`
    `not validly made: the witness is not an authorised witness`
    `not validly made: I did not sign or mark it`
    `not validly made: I did not sign or initial an alteration`
    `not validly made: I did not declare orally in the witness's presence`
    `not validly made: the witness did not sign or mark it`
    `not validly made: the witness did not sign or initial my alterations`
    `not validly made: the witness did not write their name and qualification`

GIVEN `d` IS A `Statutory declaration`
GIVETH A `How my statutory declaration stands`
DECIDE `the first unmet limb of s 12` IS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `d`'s `it is in the Schedule 1 form`)
            THEN `not validly made: not in the Schedule 1 form`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `the witness is an authorised witness`)
            THEN `not validly made: the witness is not an authorised witness`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `I signed or personally marked it`)
            THEN `not validly made: I did not sign or mark it`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `I signed or initialled every alteration`)
            THEN `not validly made: I did not sign or initial an alteration`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `I declared the s 12(3)(c) matters orally in the witness's presence`)
            THEN `not validly made: I did not declare orally in the witness's presence`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `the witness then signed or personally marked it`)
            THEN `not validly made: the witness did not sign or mark it`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `the witness signed or initialled my alterations`)
            THEN `not validly made: the witness did not sign or initial my alterations`
        IF (NOT `d`'s `the witness wrote their name and qualification`)
            THEN `not validly made: the witness did not write their name and qualification`
        OTHERWISE `validly made under s 12`

-- The limbs are checked in the order of s 12; the first that fails is reported unless s 16 saves the departure.
@export Is my WA statutory declaration validly made (ss 12, 16)?
GIVEN `my declaration` IS A `Statutory declaration` @desc How your declaration was prepared, declared and witnessed
GIVETH A `How my statutory declaration stands`
DECIDE `Is my statutory declaration validly made` IS
    CONSIDER `the first unmet limb of s 12` `my declaration`
    WHEN `validly made under s 12` THEN `validly made under s 12`
    OTHERWISE
        IF `my declaration`'s `any departure is saved by s 16`
        THEN `valid despite a departure: s 16 saves it`
        ELSE `the first unmet limb of s 12` `my declaration`

§§ `7(3), 9(3), 12(3) -- what the Act says about witnessing remotely or electronically`

-- s 7(3): "The person (A) administering an oath or taking the affirmation of another person (B) by means of an audio link
-- or a video link, within the meaning of those terms in the Evidence Act 1906 section 120, may do so in as nearly as
-- practicable the same way as if B were in the presence of A."
-- s 9(3)(c) and s 12(3)(c) require the maker to swear, affirm or declare "in the presence of an authorised witness".
-- Neither says whether presence by audio or video link will do, and the Act says nothing about electronic signatures or
-- electronic documents. s 7(3) speaks of administering an oath or taking an affirmation; whether an authorised witness to
-- an affidavit is doing that, so that s 7(3) reaches the affidavit oath, the Act does not say. The encoding returns that
-- the Act does not settle it, rather than guess.
DECLARE `Kind of act` IS ONE OF
    `swearing an oath or making an affirmation`
    `swearing or affirming an affidavit`
    `making a statutory declaration`
    `signing my statutory declaration or affidavit electronically`

DECLARE `What this Act says about doing it remotely` IS ONE OF
    `yes: s 7(3) lets the oath or affirmation be taken by audio or video link`
    `not settled by this Act: s 9(3)(c) needs the witness's presence, and s 7(3) may or may not reach the affidavit oath`
    `not settled by this Act: s 12(3)(c) needs the witness's presence, and no provision speaks of a link for a declaration`
    `not settled by this Act: it says nothing about electronic signatures`

@export Does this Act let me do this by audio or video link, or electronically (ss 7(3), 9(3)(c), 12(3)(c))?
GIVEN `what I want to do` IS A `Kind of act` @desc What you want to do remotely or electronically
GIVETH A `What this Act says about doing it remotely`
DECIDE `Does the Act let me do it remotely` IS
    CONSIDER `what I want to do`
    WHEN `swearing an oath or making an affirmation` THEN `yes: s 7(3) lets the oath or affirmation be taken by audio or video link`
    WHEN `swearing or affirming an affidavit` THEN `not settled by this Act: s 9(3)(c) needs the witness's presence, and s 7(3) may or may not reach the affidavit oath`
    WHEN `making a statutory declaration` THEN `not settled by this Act: s 12(3)(c) needs the witness's presence, and no provision speaks of a link for a declaration`
    OTHERWISE `not settled by this Act: it says nothing about electronic signatures`

§§ `13 -- as the witness, when the maker is blind or illiterate`

-- s 13(1): "If the person making an affidavit or statutory declaration is blind or illiterate, the authorised witness for
-- the document must -- (a) read the document aloud to the person, or cause [it] to be read aloud ... in the authorised
-- witness's presence; and (b) satisfy himself or herself that the person understood what was read aloud; and (c) certify
-- on the document" both things. s 13(2): this is "additional to section 9 or 12".
@export As the witness, must I read the document aloud, check it was understood, and certify that on it (s 13)?
GIVEN `the maker is blind or illiterate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person making the affidavit or declaration is blind or illiterate
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Must I read it aloud and certify` IF
    `the maker is blind or illiterate`

§§ `14, 15 -- will a court accept my document`

-- s 14(1): a maker not sufficiently conversant with English may make an affidavit in another language, "but the
-- affidavit is not admissible in a court or by a person acting judicially unless -- (a) the affidavit is translated into
-- written English by a suitably qualified translator; and (b) the translator makes an affidavit" setting out
-- qualifications, saying the translation is accurate, and attaching it. s 14(2) applies the same to a statutory
-- declaration.
-- s 15(1): the maker or witness "must not use a rubber or other stamp to make the person's signature or personal mark."
-- s 15(2): a stamp may still imprint the name near the signature. s 15(3): a document "signed by the maker or witness
-- with a rubber stamp is not admissible in a court or by a person acting judicially."
-- Only ss 14 and 15 are encoded: "admissible as far as ss 14 and 15 go" says nothing of the rules of evidence.
DECLARE `Admissibility under ss 14 and 15` IS ONE OF
    `ss 14 and 15 do not stand in the way`
    `not admissible: signed with a rubber stamp (s 15(3))`
    `not admissible: no English translation with a translator's affidavit (s 14)`

@export Do ss 14 and 15 stop a court accepting my affidavit or statutory declaration?
GIVEN `the maker or witness signed with a rubber stamp` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a stamp was used to make a signature or mark (not merely to imprint a name)
      `it is in a language other than English` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it has a qualified translation and translator's affidavit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a suitably qualified translator translated it into written English and made an affidavit with the translation attached
GIVETH A `Admissibility under ss 14 and 15`
DECIDE `Do ss 14 and 15 stop a court accepting it` IS
    BRANCH
        IF `the maker or witness signed with a rubber stamp`
            THEN `not admissible: signed with a rubber stamp (s 15(3))`
        IF `it is in a language other than English` AND (NOT `it has a qualified translation and translator's affidavit`)
            THEN `not admissible: no English translation with a translator's affidavit (s 14)`
        OTHERWISE `ss 14 and 15 do not stand in the way`

@export May I use a rubber stamp to imprint my name beside my signature (s 15(2))?
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `May I stamp my name beside my signature` IS TRUE

§§ `17 -- pretending to be an authorised witness`

-- s 17: "If a person who is not an authorised witness for affidavits or for statutory declarations pretends to be, or
-- asserts that he or she is, such an authorised witness in the knowledge that he or she is not, the person commits an
-- offence. Penalty: Imprisonment for 12 months."
@export Do I commit the s 17 offence of pretending to be an authorised witness?
GIVEN `I am an authorised witness` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you are in fact an authorised witness for the affidavit or declaration
      `I pretended or asserted that I am one` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I knew I was not one` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I commit the pretending offence` IF
        (NOT `I am an authorised witness`)
    AND `I pretended or asserted that I am one`
    AND `I knew I was not one`

@export What is the longest I can be imprisoned for pretending to be an authorised witness (s 17), in months?
GIVETH A NUMBER
DECIDE `What is the longest I can be imprisoned for pretending in months` IS 12

§ `Criminal Code (WA) s 169 -- a false statement in my affidavit or declaration`

-- Schedule 1 makes the declarant say: "I know that it is an offence to make a declaration knowing that it is false in a
-- material particular." The offence is in the Criminal Code, not this Act.
-- Criminal Code s 169(1): "Any person who, when under oath or any sanction that may be lawfully substituted for an oath,
-- knowingly makes a statement, whether orally or in writing, that is false in a material particular is guilty of a crime
-- and is liable to imprisonment for 7 years. Summary conviction penalty: imprisonment for 3 years and a fine of $36 000."
-- s 169(2): "Any person who knowingly makes a statement that is false in a material particular in a statutory
-- declaration is guilty of a crime and is liable to imprisonment for 5 years. Summary conviction penalty: imprisonment for
-- 2 years and a fine of $24 000."
-- An affidavit is sworn or affirmed (s 9(3)(c)); an affirmation is a sanction lawfully substituted for an oath (s 5(3)).
DECLARE `Kind of document` IS ONE OF
    `my statutory declaration`
    `my affidavit, sworn or affirmed`

DECLARE `Most I face` HAS
    `years of imprisonment on indictment` IS A NUMBER
    `years of imprisonment on summary conviction` IS A NUMBER
    `dollars of fine on summary conviction` IS A NUMBER

@export Do I commit a crime under Criminal Code s 169 by what I said in my affidavit or declaration?
GIVEN `I made a statement in it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the statement is false in a material particular` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I knew it was false` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `Do I commit the false statement crime` IF
        `I made a statement in it`
    AND `the statement is false in a material particular`
    AND `I knew it was false`

@export What is the most I face for a knowingly false statement (Criminal Code s 169)?
GIVEN `the document` IS A `Kind of document` @desc Whether the false statement was in a statutory declaration or in an affidavit
GIVETH A `Most I face`
DECIDE `What is the most I face for a false statement` IS
    CONSIDER `the document`
    WHEN `my statutory declaration` THEN `Most I face` WITH
        `years of imprisonment on indictment` IS 5
        `years of imprisonment on summary conviction` IS 2
        `dollars of fine on summary conviction` IS 24000
    OTHERWISE `Most I face` WITH
        `years of imprisonment on indictment` IS 7
        `years of imprisonment on summary conviction` IS 3
        `dollars of fine on summary conviction` IS 36000
